  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Prefrontal trough right ahead of a strong cold\nfront is currently over the inner coastal plain and is slowly \nprogressing eastward. This is bringing robust forcing into a\nregion with around 1kJ/kg of CAPE, deep shear of 30-35 knots,\nand increasing moisture. Dewpoints are now in the low 70s for\nportions of eastern NC, especially east of hwy 17. This has\nincreased the available moisture/instability, and as a result\nthe threat of severe weather has INCREASED for this afternoon\nand evening inland. SPC has upgraded portions of the CWA to a\n\"Slight\" (2/5) risk of severe weather. Main concerns are\ndamaging wind gusts up to 60 mph. Some small hail with the\ncooler air aloft and mid-level lapse rates 6-7 C/km can't be\nruled out. In addition, there is a low-end risk for a brief,\nweak, isolated tornado or two. Areas that have more backed winds\nout of the SSE or SE as the front approaches would see a more\npronounced chance of tornado formation as that would locally\nenhance low level SRH values. \n\nWe could see localized QPF values of >1.5\", but at this point\nflooding potential is low as we have been very dry as of late.\n\nThe main threat window runs from early afternoon to around \nsunset inland, lingering slightly longer over the Outer Banks \nbut with a diminishing severe risk as instability collapses. The\nmain front sweeps offshore late tonight with any remaining \nshowers exiting the OBX around or shortly after midnight. There \nwill likely be a delay between the main front coming through and\nthe dry air surge, so would not be surprised to see a period of\nlow stratus Fri night into Sat morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A more seasonably dry airmass will be ushered \nin for the weekend behind the front. Expect a return to mostly \nsunny skies and highs in the upper 60s to low 70s Sat, then \ncooler yet for Sun with 60-65. Lows will be in the 40s, with \nsome 30s for some of the normally colder spots. We will have to \nmonitor the temp forecast for any frost concerns Sunday morning.",
